                  "Text": "This simulation was setup based on a defoliation trial at Scott farm, Hamilton, New Zealand, run from 2010 to 2012 by DairyNZ.  The study investigated the effect of cut height (which define rotation length) and residual height on chicory and plantain growth.  The treatments consisted of trigerring cuts when the sward reached target heights of 150, 250, 350, and 450 mm, in factorial combination with two residual heights, 30-50 or 60-80 mm.  Harvest was avoided over winter.  Note that not always the target height was reached.  \r\nTrial plots were sown with plantain, cultivar '*Ceres Tonic*' (at 10 kg/ha), after previous pasture was sprayed with herbicide and conventionally drilled.  Irrigation was applied in the first year and fertiliser was applied over summer.  Results reported include biomass yield, botanical composition (presence of weeds), plant density, leaf area, and light interception (the later two only during one growing period - between two cuts).  \r\n\r\nReferences:  \r\nLee, J.M.;  Hemmingson, N.R.; Minnee, E.M.K.; Clark, C.E.F. 2015. Management strategies for chicory (Cichorium intybus) and plantain (Plantago lanceolata): impact on dry matter yield, nutritive characteristics and plant density. Crop and Pasture Science, 66, 168–183.\r\n",
                  "Name": "Memo",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [],
                  "Enabled": true,
                  "ReadOnly": false
                },
